-- 002_domain_schema.sql.todo
--
-- THIS FILE IS INTENTIONALLY NOT RUN. The migrate runner only picks up *.sql.
--
-- Your Week 1 deliverable lives here, and it is yours to design — no schema is
-- provided on purpose, because designing it is the entire lesson.
--
-- How to use it:
--   1. Rename this file to 002_domain_schema.sql
--      (git mv db/migrations/002_domain_schema.sql.todo db/migrations/002_domain_schema.sql)
--   2. Write your tables.
--   3. Run: npm run db:migrate
--   4. Run: npm run db:migrate again — it should say "0 applied". Migrations must
--      be safe to run twice on an already-migrated database.
--
-- Requirements for the schema (ROADMAP.md -> Week 1):
--   [ ] 6–10 tables for one realistic domain you care about
--   [ ] Primary keys, foreign keys, NOT NULL, UNIQUE and CHECK constraints
--   [ ] Money as integer cents or numeric — never float
--   [ ] Timestamps as timestamptz, with sensible defaults
--   [ ] Soft-delete or status columns where they genuinely matter
--   [ ] A second migration that ALTERs an existing table (prove you can evolve it)
--   [ ] A seed script with enough rows (10k+) to make indexes measurable
--
-- Deliberately NOT included, because Week 2 is where they belong:
--   endpoints, auth tables, tenant scoping.
--
-- Pick a domain with real rules — bookings, invoices, inventory, a clinic, a
-- small marketplace. "Users and posts" teaches you nothing about constraints.
